WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:06.270
SAMI ZUHURUDDIN:
Thank you for coming.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.670
My name is Sami Zuhuruddin.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:12.780
I am a Solutions Architect
on the Google Cloud team.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.980
Just a quick show
of hands, who here--

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.680
this talk is mainly geared
towards the decisionmakers--

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.370
CEOs, leaders, IT execs.

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:27.700
Who here is here because
they are in that capacity?

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.540
OK, great.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.610
Who's here because the
ML session was full?

00:00:35.247 --> 00:00:36.080
That's why I'm here.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.710
All right, so, now that we
know each other so well--

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.310
you know about me,
I know about you--

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.920
I'd like to admit
something here right

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.852
at the outset, which
is fine once you

00:00:49.852 --> 00:00:50.810
know a group of people.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:53.660
So I'd like to admit
that about 10 years ago I

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.180
worked for a startup.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.800
That's not what
I'm here to admit.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.212
Who hasn't worked for a startup?

00:00:59.212 --> 00:01:00.920
That's a more relevant
question nowadays.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.253
Who hasn't worked for a startup?

00:01:02.253 --> 00:01:03.710
OK, cool, about five of you.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:10.040
But, anyways, I had a serious
problem about 10 years ago

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.865
working for a startup.

00:01:12.865 --> 00:01:14.240
I used to rack
and stack servers.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.571
It went part and parcel
with the job back then.

00:01:20.571 --> 00:01:21.320
It was satisfying.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.986
You'd look at servers,
you'd stack them,

00:01:22.986 --> 00:01:25.770
or you'd rack and stack them,
you'd turn around, it's done.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.860
Of course, when I racked
and stacked servers,

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:31.400
they did not look this good.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.590
But like what happens with
every startup, we got bought.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:38.479
That's pretty much what
happens with every startup--

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:39.020
most of them.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:40.670
A couple of them
don't get bought.

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.590
But things calmed down
and I had a chance

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.680
to reflect in a
new organization.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:46.530
Things calmed down.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.196
In any startup, things
are very chaotic,

00:01:48.196 --> 00:01:49.160
but things calmed down.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.950
You look at the patterns
you've been engaging on

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.240
and decide what's next.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:54.110
How do you optimize?

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:55.500
How do you clean things up?

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.850
There's a lot of technical debt.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:00.560
And the idea of
racking servers, I

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.280
was like there has
to be a better way.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.260
And, of course, what was coming
and prevailing on the scene

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:09.110
at the time was you can get
away from racking and stacking

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:11.180
servers by interacting
with an API.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.136
And I thought that
was pretty cool stuff.

00:02:14.136 --> 00:02:16.760
As much as I enjoyed racking and
stacking servers and deploying

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.787
things by hand, nothing
was quite as easy

00:02:18.787 --> 00:02:20.870
as issuing a command, and
then all of a sudden you

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:23.990
have resources at your disposal.

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:29.630
So things winded down
for me in terms of what

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.246
I was doing as an IT leader.

00:02:31.246 --> 00:02:32.495
And I was like, you know what?

00:02:32.495 --> 00:02:35.090
I think this cloud
thing is pretty cool.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.594
I'm going to go and--

00:02:36.594 --> 00:02:38.510
I saw a job description,
a solution architect.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:39.051
What is this?

00:02:39.051 --> 00:02:41.310
You go talk to customers.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.490
That sounds great.

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.441
You tell them the
cloud is great.

00:02:44.441 --> 00:02:45.440
I'm like, I can do that.

00:02:46.844 --> 00:02:48.260
And I was like,
yeah, that's easy.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:51.470
I mean, it's like
a total easy sale.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:52.900
So I thought that'd be great.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.500
I'd talk to people like myself
and I'll spread the word.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.670
And what I found, of
course, was that as soon

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:00.950
as I started having
conversations,

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:04.400
a lot of the IT executives
are like not so fast.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.920
You don't know anything.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.190
And what I found is it turns
out that IT leadership is just

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:13.700
spread across a huge spectrum.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.100
And they have really
varied reactions

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:20.020
to the idea of
going to the cloud.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:23.240
And generally, time has
caused this to shift over.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.110
A few years ago, most
of the conversations

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:29.693
are about awareness, and what's
possible, and stuff like that.

00:03:29.693 --> 00:03:33.290
Now it's generally about
strategy, execution, costs,

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.660
picking a cloud, picking
a technology that you

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:39.110
can use to move to the cloud.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:40.850
It's about cloud strategy.

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:44.510
Everybody today has
a cloud strategy,

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.610
except for those who don't.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:48.820
But they all say they
have a cloud strategy.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:50.330
It's just some of them are
in research and some of them

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:50.830
are not.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.396
But it's not enough
to simply say, hey,

00:03:56.396 --> 00:03:57.770
we're going to
move to the cloud.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.660
That's necessary,
but not sufficient.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:07.390
And that's rightfully so.

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:10.990
As you move towards a CIO
organization, there's sort of--

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:12.160
they're dealing with a lot.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.490
There's security breaches, and
capex, and how to train folks.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:19.540
And so, it's rightfully
cautious and concerned.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:22.029
There's a lot at stake.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.340
And this is the way it's
come together for me.

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:25.540
This is my grouping.

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:28.930
This is loosely resembling
the "Crossing the Chasm"

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.730
from Geoffrey Moore.

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:32.560
But generally what
I find is there's

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.880
sort of this huge break on
this spectrum, which is folks

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:39.910
that are at that end of it.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:42.280
They haven't touched cloud
much, and it takes a lot

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.911
to get them off that path.

00:04:44.911 --> 00:04:46.910
And then there's some
that warm up to your idea,

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:48.450
and they'll say, oh,
we'll do dev and test.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:48.950
Why?

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:52.060
Because that's kind
of a throw away--

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:54.730
or it's an easy one to look at.

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:57.430
But the big moment is when you
do that first production app.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.690
I find that that's
generally the first time

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:03.460
that the light bulbs
go off to where

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:05.050
this is something that's real.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.940
Usually, I find when
folks cross that chasm,

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:09.702
things accelerate
very, very fast.

00:05:09.702 --> 00:05:11.410
And you start talking
about hybrid cloud.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:13.030
We want to bridge
environments together.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:14.050
We want to bridge
networks together.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:15.640
We want cloud
environment to look

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:18.190
like the on-prem environment.

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:21.940
And the evolution
of that is folks

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:23.210
say, OK, we're going to exit.

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:24.280
We're going to exit on-prem.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:25.978
We're going to have a three year
strategy, a five year strategy

00:05:25.978 --> 00:05:27.440
to be all inside the cloud.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.690
And there's so many of
these success stories.

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:31.352
I call them success stories.

00:05:31.352 --> 00:05:33.810
I don't know if hardware vendors
call them success stories,

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.590
but I call them success stories.

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:36.840
And then there's cloud native.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:39.010
Cloud native is this term that
means that, you know what?

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:41.140
You're looking at APIs, and
you're looking at technologies

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.670
and saying how do we
actually build and rebuild

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:44.860
our applications and redesign
them to take advantage

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.480
of these platforms?

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:53.380
And so, for someone
in my position,

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.840
it makes a lot of sense
to try to interpret

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:02.170
the signals that you hear from
folks across that spectrum.

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:09.160
And so, today's talk is
generally about cloud adoption,

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:11.770
but it's specifically
about Google Cloud.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:15.060
And it's a collection of
feedback that I've seen.

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.567
It's a collection of direct
feedback from customers

00:06:17.567 --> 00:06:18.400
and lessons learned.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.620
Some of the customers
are actually here.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.560
And so, I tried to make the
talk divided in two parts.

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:25.060
One of it is just
observations I see

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:28.810
about what makes cloud
endeavors very successful inside

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.430
of customer organizations.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:33.100
And the other is about what
folks on top of Google Cloud

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:37.680
have told me made a
difference to their journey.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.970
And so, I try to interpret
the signals I get,

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:41.470
because I'd like
to figure out where

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:43.660
folks are on that spectrum.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:46.870
And this is a very
telling signal

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:50.200
that someone's on the very
early part of the spectrum.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:51.730
I, myself, used to say this.

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:53.950
This is a very kind
of safety setting.

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:55.537
But essentially, it's a--

00:06:55.537 --> 00:06:57.370
hey, nobody ever got
fired for, and then you

00:06:57.370 --> 00:06:58.453
pick your favorite vendor.

00:06:58.453 --> 00:07:01.060
And it's sort of like a--
it's a very safe place,

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.667
but it tells me where
somebody is on the spectrum

00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:06.250
and what kind of discussions are
going to be relevant to them.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:07.540
And I, myself-- like I said--

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.940
have said this in the
past, maybe 10 years ago.

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:13.210
But it tells me that we need to
challenge conventional wisdom.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:17.030
I apologize here.

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:38.195
Sorry about that.

00:07:38.195 --> 00:07:41.560
The irony is I know CEOs
who used to say this.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:44.143
And by the time they
realized that this

00:07:44.143 --> 00:07:47.430
was more prohibitive than
it wasn't prohibitive,

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:50.650
it was too late for them
or their organizations,

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:53.740
and it required drastic
change to move out of it.

00:08:00.932 --> 00:08:01.890
This is another signal.

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:03.556
This is another signal,
a different kind

00:08:03.556 --> 00:08:05.880
of CIO, a different
kind of IT executive

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:07.130
where they sort of get it.

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:08.340
They sort of know.

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:11.220
Eventually, they can see
that the end of this horizon

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:13.140
is to end up on the
other side of it.

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:15.675
And this is usually
a great indicator,

00:08:15.675 --> 00:08:18.792
a great signal that they're
looking at their environment

00:08:18.792 --> 00:08:19.500
very differently.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:21.960
They're like, the folks I
have today or the skills

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:24.180
that I have today
aren't quite going

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:25.920
to align with that
cloud journey.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:27.210
We need to rethink this.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:30.390
We need to foster new skills.

00:08:30.390 --> 00:08:32.130
And so, it tells
me that they see

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:34.510
that that's the end
of the destination,

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:38.730
but they're not really
sure how to incubate that.

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:43.289
What I find interesting about
that is the skills like--

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:46.031
OK, networking is still a skill.

00:08:46.031 --> 00:08:47.530
Folks that know
networking are still

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:50.610
required in a cloud
environment, but the way

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:53.190
they're going to do networking
changes dramatically.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:56.370
And so, what I advocate is
if somebody knows networking,

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:57.490
that's 100% applicable.

00:08:57.490 --> 00:08:59.240
If somebody knows
application development,

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:00.960
that's 100% applicable.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:03.370
But what we need to do is
educate on the fact of--

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:04.800
or train on the
fact of how do you

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.995
use API-driven infrastructure if
folks haven't used code before,

00:09:08.995 --> 00:09:10.620
if folks haven't used
automation tools.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:11.880
These are the things
we need to bolster on.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:13.170
But the fundamental
concepts about how

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:15.390
do you secure an environment,
how networking works,

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:17.350
how application development
works, how storage works,

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:18.725
these things don't
really change.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:27.532
Regardless of which side
of the spectrum you're on,

00:09:27.532 --> 00:09:29.740
by and large, this is the
first thing that comes out.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:32.355
We don't want to lock-in.

00:09:32.355 --> 00:09:34.605
And it's not really an
objection about moving forward.

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:39.310
Even if somebody
has decided going

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:45.030
cloud is completely the way
to go, lock-in is a huge fear.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:47.760
And the reality is, there's
two kinds of lock-in.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:51.090
This is the way I've broken
down this discussion.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:54.180
It's what makes sense to me, and
I think it comes across well.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:55.350
There's hard lock-in.

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:57.480
Hard lock-in is
the kind of lock-in

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:01.890
where you're in an environment,
you're in a contract,

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:04.740
and there's pain
or financial waste

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:11.179
for backtracking or ceasing
that use of that contract.

00:10:11.179 --> 00:10:12.720
And so, you're pretty
much locked-in.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:13.844
You're like, you know what?

00:10:13.844 --> 00:10:14.670
We signed this.

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:17.820
We're committed for
the next three years

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:21.120
to use this environment,
use this technology.

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:25.220
And so, it has this
negative connotation to it.

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:26.720
In reality, these
are something else

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:29.550
that I think that we
don't pay attention to.

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:31.450
There's something
called soft lock-in.

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:34.170
Soft lock-in, to me, is
much, much more relevant.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:35.880
Soft lock-in is
all the decisions

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.700
you make above the hardware.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.250
So picking a database,
picking an application stack,

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:49.740
picking an application framework
has a much longer tail from a--

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:53.100
has a much longer tail, from
an environment perspective,

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:54.950
than the hard lock-in.

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:56.730
And what I mean by
that is you don't

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:58.814
tend to change your
database on a dime.

00:10:58.814 --> 00:11:01.230
You don't tend to change your
application stack on a dime.

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:04.320
And so, what you want
to do is spend time

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:09.720
figuring out how to make
this soft lock-in choice

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:11.730
in a way that gives you
the most flexibility.

00:11:11.730 --> 00:11:14.430
And this is what's going
to sound paradoxical

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:19.410
is, how do you make a soft
lock-in choice that makes sure

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:21.420
that you're open to
a lot of choices?

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:23.920
And the way to do that is
to pick open standards--

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:26.550
open standards, open
APIs, open source.

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:28.440
And this is why Google
spends a lot of time

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:29.802
in this dimension around that.

00:11:29.802 --> 00:11:31.260
And it's going to
seem paradoxical,

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:33.480
but if you make the right
soft lock-in choices,

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:37.800
it really gives you
a lot of leverage

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:40.470
to avoid getting stuck in
a hard lock-in situation.

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:42.482
And we'll talk more
about that in the future.

00:11:42.482 --> 00:11:44.190
And the best way to
do this, the best way

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:48.150
to pick a soft lock-in
option is to look

00:11:48.150 --> 00:11:50.970
at where the trajectory
is of a certain technology

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:54.912
in open source frameworks
that are being released.

00:11:54.912 --> 00:11:56.370
Figure out where
are they going, do

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:59.066
they align with the
development objectives we have?

00:11:59.066 --> 00:12:00.690
And if we choose
those, does it give us

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:02.940
the most flexibility to
avoid being hard locked

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:05.460
in to an environment?

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:08.059
So they're intertwined,
but I often

00:12:08.059 --> 00:12:10.350
find that folks just get
stuck on the hard lock-in when

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:13.410
they just want a
bunch of parachutes

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:15.210
to get out of an environment.

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:17.100
And that sometimes
is a hindrance,

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:18.510
because you're spending
more time figuring out

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:20.550
how to make sure you're not
locked in to an environment

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:22.320
than figuring out how
to make choices that

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:23.280
allow you to build your stack.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:25.321
At the end of the day, we
have to build software.

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:36.360
So what I'd like to do instead
is to step back and say,

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:38.219
what kind of
organization are you?

00:12:38.219 --> 00:12:39.885
That's a far more
relevant conversation.

00:12:39.885 --> 00:12:43.169
If we get stuck down into
making exact technical choices,

00:12:43.169 --> 00:12:45.210
then I feel like we dive
into the weeds too fast.

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:48.930
Now, I like ask are
you a product company,

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:52.410
are you a service company,
are you a data company?

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:54.787
And not focusing on what
kind of business you are

00:12:54.787 --> 00:12:57.540
is missing the
forest for the trees.

00:12:57.540 --> 00:12:59.340
And the best
conversations are the ones

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:01.650
that get down into what
kind of company are you,

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:03.420
what do you want to do?

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:06.210
And then it becomes clear
what is the purpose of your IT

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:07.947
organization.

00:13:07.947 --> 00:13:10.530
When it becomes clear what the
purpose of your IT organization

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:15.120
is, to me, I like having
this revelation moment.

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:17.700
The commonality in all of those
situations is it's software.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:19.110
Your IT organization
is basically

00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:20.729
helping enable software.

00:13:20.729 --> 00:13:22.770
And that software could
be driving your business.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:24.390
That software could be
the crux of your business.

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:26.098
It could be the product
of your business.

00:13:26.098 --> 00:13:28.566
It could be making what you
produce as a company software

00:13:28.566 --> 00:13:29.066
better.

00:13:29.066 --> 00:13:31.650
There isn't anything in
terms of an IT organization

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:33.999
that isn't made better
by better software that's

00:13:33.999 --> 00:13:35.790
produced faster-- not
just faster software.

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:37.498
That's just better
software, but software

00:13:37.498 --> 00:13:40.684
that you can produce
faster at a higher quality.

00:13:40.684 --> 00:13:42.600
And that's why it's
relevant to be here today.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:45.780
If this was a manufacturing
conference and you

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:48.060
were a medical
device manufacturer,

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:52.080
you'd be talking about making
the manufacturing process more

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:54.550
efficient, because that's
critical to your business.

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:56.280
But when you're at a technical
conference like this,

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:58.050
and when you're figuring out
what technologies to use,

00:13:58.050 --> 00:13:59.850
what cloud to use,
obviously, it's

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:02.010
about those
technologies in helping

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:04.170
you make better software.

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:08.220
And so, as it turns out,
Google has a little bit

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:11.930
of experience with software.

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:14.100
This was our original mission.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:19.860
And from the beginning, Google
has been about, essentially,

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:23.190
harnessing the world's data,
making it globally, universally

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:25.350
accessible and meaningful.

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:28.890
And to do that, you have to
learn a lot about dealing

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:31.200
with data at scale.

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:33.090
And we've done a lot
of things to figure out

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:35.400
how to deal with data at scale.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:41.030
And the slides keep
going off sync.

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:41.530
Sorry.

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:52.510
And that's resulted in two
aspects of Google's forte.

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:54.985
Google has technical leadership
that we'll talk about

00:14:54.985 --> 00:14:56.320
and infrastructure leadership.

00:14:56.320 --> 00:14:59.620
And if we didn't develop new
mechanisms and techniques

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:01.630
for dealing with
data at scale, they

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:04.330
wouldn't have led to the
revelations, and research,

00:15:04.330 --> 00:15:06.976
and infrastructure that we
have in order to power that.

00:15:06.976 --> 00:15:08.350
This is an incredible
feat, to be

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:10.397
able to deal with
data at the volume

00:15:10.397 --> 00:15:11.980
where you can be
sitting on your phone

00:15:11.980 --> 00:15:13.771
right here-- as a great
number of you are--

00:15:13.771 --> 00:15:15.460
and surfing your mail.

00:15:15.460 --> 00:15:19.930
That's something
that we have had

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:23.410
to break the mold of what was
conventionally possible before.

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:25.492
But when we say Google
is a technical leader

00:15:25.492 --> 00:15:26.950
or an infrastructure
leader, that's

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:29.920
not meant to be an
exclusionary term.

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:33.100
Google, in fact,
puts out thousands

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:34.630
of papers of research.

00:15:34.630 --> 00:15:35.942
This is an example of--

00:15:35.942 --> 00:15:37.150
you can go to this right now.

00:15:37.150 --> 00:15:38.980
I mean, if you want
to, literally right now

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:40.240
you could go to this.

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:45.040
And papers have come out
on GFS, HDFS, MapReduce.

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:47.290
And that is directly,
essentially,

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:50.560
the impetus behind Hadoop.

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:52.780
Bigtable is the
impetus behind HBase--

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:58.490
Dremel is the inspiration
behind Apache Drill,

00:15:58.490 --> 00:15:59.974
Borg, and so on and so forth.

00:15:59.974 --> 00:16:01.390
And so, from
Google's perspective,

00:16:01.390 --> 00:16:03.765
yes, we essentially have had
to solve all these problems.

00:16:03.765 --> 00:16:05.380
We've put all
these problems out.

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:07.975
We put the solutions of these
problems out on the internet.

00:16:07.975 --> 00:16:09.850
So when I talked earlier
about open standards

00:16:09.850 --> 00:16:12.160
and open interfaces,
we've done a lot

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:15.160
to make sure that,
as a community,

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:18.085
you're enabled with
the same assets.

00:16:18.085 --> 00:16:20.710
And they've resulted in some of
the projects that have affected

00:16:20.710 --> 00:16:22.240
how data is managed today.

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:24.220
And I don't where the
data community would

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:27.004
be without things like Hadoop.

00:16:27.004 --> 00:16:30.637
A second token of
that is open source.

00:16:30.637 --> 00:16:32.220
So when I talked
about research, these

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:34.280
are things that we put
out in the form of papers.

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:35.700
But open source, these
are direct projects

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:37.859
that we're, essentially,
grooming, and curating,

00:16:37.859 --> 00:16:39.150
and producing to the community.

00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:43.860
And technologies like Go, and
Angular, and TensorFlow, these

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:46.530
are technologies that are
part of infrastructure stacks

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:48.321
and software development
stacks that you're

00:16:48.321 --> 00:16:50.710
using today, currently.

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:52.906
And so, when I talk
about our research

00:16:52.906 --> 00:16:54.280
and I talk about
our open source,

00:16:54.280 --> 00:16:57.090
this is an example of what I
deem the technical leadership,

00:16:57.090 --> 00:17:00.930
is that Google is pushing the
bounds to solve our mission,

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:02.874
to bring that
mission to reality.

00:17:02.874 --> 00:17:04.290
We're pushing the
bounds of what's

00:17:04.290 --> 00:17:06.510
possible with data at
scale, and we're simply

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:07.740
sharing that with the world.

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:09.540
We're putting that out there.

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:15.790
But our technical leadership
doesn't stand on its own.

00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:22.180
So it takes an enormous
amount of infrastructure,

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:24.901
in the form of data centers,
and compute, and storage,

00:17:24.901 --> 00:17:25.400
and network.

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:28.930
It takes a tremendous amount
of technical infrastructure

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:32.697
to support, essentially, the
open source or the published

00:17:32.697 --> 00:17:34.030
software that I've talked about.

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:38.380
And so, that becomes the other
pillar of the Google dynamic.

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:41.280
It's what we put out in terms
of our thought leadership

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:44.560
and what we put out in terms of
our infrastructure leadership.

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:51.850
And I sort of mentioned this
as a buildup to the fact

00:17:51.850 --> 00:17:53.494
that Google makes sure--

00:17:53.494 --> 00:17:54.910
or the way we work
internally-- we

00:17:54.910 --> 00:17:58.300
try to make sure that developers
are enabled and empowered.

00:17:58.300 --> 00:18:01.760
We don't want developers to deal
with very low level operations.

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:04.051
We don't want developers to
have to think about logging

00:18:04.051 --> 00:18:04.600
into servers.

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:07.016
We want developers to have as
little friction as they can,

00:18:07.016 --> 00:18:10.390
because we want them to make
better software, faster.

00:18:10.390 --> 00:18:13.570
And so, we've figured out
how to abstract hardware,

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:15.590
like the idea of
notion of servers,

00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:18.316
and where you should
store data, and where

00:18:18.316 --> 00:18:19.690
you should backup
things, and how

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:20.470
networks should be structured.

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:22.750
We've abstracted all that
away so the developers

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:25.162
can have a very fast
clip of moving forward.

00:18:25.162 --> 00:18:26.620
And if they can
have a faster clip,

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:30.640
they can improve software
development, times, quality,

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:33.656
and make it faster.

00:18:33.656 --> 00:18:35.156
So why do I spend
so much time here?

00:18:35.156 --> 00:18:36.620
This wasn't meant
to be a sales pitch,

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:38.786
but I want you to understand
where Google is coming.

00:18:38.786 --> 00:18:41.290
Wayne Gretzky says skate
to where the puck is going.

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:46.060
That was echoed probably about
1,200 times this morning.

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:47.950
But it's an important
notion to think

00:18:47.950 --> 00:18:50.320
about is that Google thinks
about these problems,

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:52.900
because it's entirely
relevant to its own mission.

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:54.510
And it makes sure
that the community--

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:55.610
it's not an exclusionary thing.

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:57.220
It makes sure that
the community is

00:18:57.220 --> 00:18:59.350
able to take advantage of that.

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:00.910
And it's entirely
relevant to you

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:03.726
being here for Google
Cloud, because if you

00:19:03.726 --> 00:19:06.100
put those two aspects together--
our technical leadership

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:07.558
and our infrastructure
leadership--

00:19:07.558 --> 00:19:10.900
and you put those as
inputs, the output of that

00:19:10.900 --> 00:19:12.720
is Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:15.220
The very same things that I've
talked about in our research,

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:18.760
in our open source, and what
was talked about all morning

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:21.640
is what is put
into Google Cloud.

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:23.519
So not only is it
something that you

00:19:23.519 --> 00:19:25.060
can go and download,
and figure out--

00:19:25.060 --> 00:19:27.700
download the research papers
and download the open source,

00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:31.660
but it's now served up in a way
that's very easily consumable.

00:19:31.660 --> 00:19:33.017
It's very curated.

00:19:33.017 --> 00:19:34.600
And that same technical
infrastructure

00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:38.860
that the $27 billion
number was about that's

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:41.050
created and crafted
around the world,

00:19:41.050 --> 00:19:42.648
that's also served up to you.

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:48.770
What does Google Cloud
Platform look right now?

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:54.430
This is the statutory
slide of our services.

00:19:54.430 --> 00:19:57.740
So this list keeps
getting bigger.

00:19:57.740 --> 00:20:00.250
There was items
announced this morning.

00:20:00.250 --> 00:20:02.200
And if you compare
it to a year ago,

00:20:02.200 --> 00:20:05.290
two years ago, this is starting
to get very, very fleshed out.

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:08.477
It's not going to fit on
one slide by next year.

00:20:08.477 --> 00:20:10.060
And this is important,
because I think

00:20:10.060 --> 00:20:13.120
some folks have a
conception of where Google

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:17.560
is in the marketplace, where it
is from a services perspective.

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:20.800
And I like to make sure
that folks know that--

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:23.140
maybe we're not doing the
best job of advocating it--

00:20:23.140 --> 00:20:26.230
but this list is directly
informed by our research,

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:28.330
the products we run
internally, and how they've

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:31.060
been brought forward to
things as simple as compute,

00:20:31.060 --> 00:20:34.052
that everybody knows, but
also things like data flow

00:20:34.052 --> 00:20:36.010
that is directly based
on internal technologies

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:37.469
that we've externalized.

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:54.060
So I'd like to get to
the practical side.

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:55.680
So I've given you
this background

00:20:55.680 --> 00:21:00.166
on how to think about
lock-in, and where Google

00:21:00.166 --> 00:21:02.040
is coming from with the
research it produces,

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:05.470
and how we look to our technical
leadership and our thought

00:21:05.470 --> 00:21:06.750
leadership.

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:08.820
But now I'd like to move
to the technical side

00:21:08.820 --> 00:21:11.130
of the talk, which is this
is all feedback I've gotten

00:21:11.130 --> 00:21:15.319
from IT executives,
from IT leaders

00:21:15.319 --> 00:21:16.860
as they've moved to
the Google Cloud.

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:18.401
Some of them are
not on Google Cloud,

00:21:18.401 --> 00:21:20.010
but this is the
feedback I've gotten.

00:21:20.010 --> 00:21:24.060
And I bucketed it into
these five general areas

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:26.250
that I feel cause
friction, or our customers

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:30.054
feel have caused friction,
or that, conversely,

00:21:30.054 --> 00:21:31.470
if they've gotten
them right, it's

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:33.090
made a much more
pleasant journey

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:34.097
to go into Google Cloud.

00:21:34.097 --> 00:21:36.180
So I'd like to go through
each one of these things

00:21:36.180 --> 00:21:37.350
and highlight just a few items.

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:39.141
There's deep dive talks
on every single one

00:21:39.141 --> 00:21:41.160
of these areas in
the other talks

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:44.000
that are going on over
the next three days.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:45.750
But if I can highlight
a few of the things

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:47.125
that I think made
a difference, I

00:21:47.125 --> 00:21:50.300
think that will make your
overall experience on coming

00:21:50.300 --> 00:21:51.270
to Google Cloud better.

00:22:08.168 --> 00:22:09.659
Go back one, please.

00:22:13.138 --> 00:22:19.680
So economics-- I hear this a
lot, is there's this unknown.

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:21.780
When you buy a server,
you know upfront how much

00:22:21.780 --> 00:22:22.571
it's going to cost.

00:22:22.571 --> 00:22:24.870
When you do lock-in into
some of these scenarios,

00:22:24.870 --> 00:22:26.370
it's kind of
refreshing, because you

00:22:26.370 --> 00:22:27.870
know exactly how
much the outlay is,

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:30.520
and how much the support
contract is going to be.

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:35.130
And then there's all
sorts of cloud studies

00:22:35.130 --> 00:22:38.100
that say this cloud is
cheaper, that cloud is cheaper.

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:41.430
And if I was to put up
a big slide that said

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:43.110
Google Cloud is cheaper--

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:44.580
which we've done--

00:22:44.580 --> 00:22:48.820
I think the gut reaction is,
well, how do you gauge that?

00:22:48.820 --> 00:22:50.010
How do you assess that?

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:51.690
How do I, as an IT
leader, assess that?

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:52.990
How do I pass that down?

00:22:52.990 --> 00:22:55.920
It's just some internal
assessment that you guys have.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:59.310
So I say trust us.

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:00.570
I'm a believable guy.

00:23:00.570 --> 00:23:02.280
But rather, what I
think is more useful

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:06.840
is to highlight the fact that
this is completely transparent.

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:09.680
You can go to three
options that I highlight.

00:23:09.680 --> 00:23:12.030
One is a pricing calculator.

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:14.089
I cringe when I
hear somebody say,

00:23:14.089 --> 00:23:16.380
well, we think the costs are
going to run away from us.

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:18.588
We think that this is going
to be way more expensive.

00:23:18.588 --> 00:23:21.450
You're hiding something.

00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:23.490
I've read this horror
story about a customer who

00:23:23.490 --> 00:23:25.920
had runaway costs
with everything

00:23:25.920 --> 00:23:28.200
that they put up in the cloud.

00:23:28.200 --> 00:23:30.270
I don't understand
how that's possible,

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:33.030
because you could plug
in every single thing

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:34.350
into our calculator.

00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:36.716
And it's really because
you can do it by workload.

00:23:36.716 --> 00:23:38.340
You can say how many
VMs is it expected

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:40.339
to take, how much bandwidth
is it going to take,

00:23:40.339 --> 00:23:41.790
how much storage do I need?

00:23:41.790 --> 00:23:44.040
And that becomes really handy
because, number one, you

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:47.070
get to highlight how much the
savings are, but number two,

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:48.030
it's savable.

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:50.477
So you can share that
around as an artifact

00:23:50.477 --> 00:23:52.560
and send that to finance,
and managers, et cetera,

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:54.720
and get budget
approval that way.

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:57.600
But it puts the onus
on you to decide

00:23:57.600 --> 00:24:01.150
if it's a worthwhile venture
or how much savings there is.

00:24:01.150 --> 00:24:03.967
There is all kinds of
internal aspects as well.

00:24:03.967 --> 00:24:06.300
So there's a pricing calculator,
there's a pricing page,

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:07.510
and there's a TCO calculator.

00:24:07.510 --> 00:24:11.550
We realize that's a very,
very common activity

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:15.240
is for you to say how do you
stack up against X, Y, Z.

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:17.560
And so, our TCO calculator
goes, basically, and says,

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:19.080
here's a particular
given workload

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:20.880
that's on our
platform, and here's

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:22.930
what it's currently running
on another platform.

00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:26.430
And so, we try to demystify
that process as much as we can.

00:24:26.430 --> 00:24:30.170
There's some interesting
aspects to the way we do--

00:24:30.170 --> 00:24:32.010
to the way we
manage our pricing.

00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:34.970
Number one is that
VMs are per minute,

00:24:34.970 --> 00:24:36.220
after a minimum of 10 minutes.

00:24:36.220 --> 00:24:37.690
And you're just
charged per minute.

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:39.481
So what that does is
it gets you very, very

00:24:39.481 --> 00:24:42.650
close to the grain of how
you're consuming a service.

00:24:42.650 --> 00:24:45.360
There's rightsizing VMs, so
you can customize the VM size

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:49.350
to the exact amount of memory
and CPU that you want to use.

00:24:49.350 --> 00:24:52.410
And we typically see
a tremendous amount

00:24:52.410 --> 00:24:55.524
of savings when folks
optimize the heck out

00:24:55.524 --> 00:24:56.940
of how they're
using Google Cloud.

00:24:56.940 --> 00:24:58.800
You can see upwards of over 50%.

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:00.540
Again, if I put up
a slide with 50%,

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:02.373
I think that meets with
a lot of skepticism.

00:25:02.373 --> 00:25:04.230
So instead, price it out.

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:06.330
Use things like
per minute billing,

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:08.982
use things like
preemptible VMs that I'll

00:25:08.982 --> 00:25:09.940
talk about in a second.

00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:19.790
Preemptible VMs--
I'll highlight that.

00:25:19.790 --> 00:25:22.470
So I talked about per minute
and I talked about other things.

00:25:22.470 --> 00:25:24.557
Preemptible VMs--
essentially this

00:25:24.557 --> 00:25:25.890
means that you can spin up a VM.

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:28.440
We could take it away
with any time within-- you

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:30.056
get it for up to 24 hours.

00:25:30.056 --> 00:25:31.680
But as long as it
keeps running, you're

00:25:31.680 --> 00:25:34.505
getting that VM on upwards
of about an 80% discount.

00:25:34.505 --> 00:25:36.630
So if you have things like
batch processing, things

00:25:36.630 --> 00:25:38.340
that run in the background,
then that's fixed.

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:40.980
You just get an 80% discount for
as long as that VM is running.

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:44.330
Again, it's still
per minute billing.

00:25:44.330 --> 00:25:46.830
And then what's not shown here
is that the pricing is always

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:49.530
trending downwards.

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:54.900
This is another one that I
hear from a lot of pricing

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:58.020
and pricing discussions.

00:25:58.020 --> 00:26:00.662
And we don't
believe in soft ROI,

00:26:00.662 --> 00:26:02.370
is usually the way
that I hear that back.

00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:03.780
And I was like, well,
did you say that or did

00:26:03.780 --> 00:26:05.310
your finance guy say that--

00:26:05.310 --> 00:26:08.040
or did your finance person
say that, I should say.

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:08.790
And I get it.

00:26:08.790 --> 00:26:11.430
It's much easier to
deal with hard ROI.

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:13.200
If I purchase A
and I purchase B,

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:15.390
I just want to know
what the delta is.

00:26:15.390 --> 00:26:22.212
But that is such a disservice,
because of the fact of--

00:26:22.212 --> 00:26:24.420
how do you account for the
fact that if your team can

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:27.750
essentially work twice as fast,
or if you can produce software

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:30.912
with half the number
of participants?

00:26:30.912 --> 00:26:32.370
These things make
a big difference.

00:26:32.370 --> 00:26:33.953
I mean, I kind of
liken it to the fact

00:26:33.953 --> 00:26:36.327
that why do companies
acquire other companies?

00:26:36.327 --> 00:26:38.160
They acquire other
companies usually because

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:39.250
of market reach, et cetera.

00:26:39.250 --> 00:26:41.624
But a lot of times it's because,
well, that small startup

00:26:41.624 --> 00:26:43.650
is like two years ahead
of us in this space.

00:26:43.650 --> 00:26:46.350
So if you buy them, it
works out better for us,

00:26:46.350 --> 00:26:48.600
because we'll get a jumpstart
into that marketplace

00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:49.344
by two years.

00:26:49.344 --> 00:26:50.760
We'll acquire all
those engineers.

00:26:50.760 --> 00:26:52.801
So we make all kinds of
decisions in our business

00:26:52.801 --> 00:26:55.470
to accelerate the
pace of business.

00:26:55.470 --> 00:26:57.900
And on the outright, I think
that you could turn around

00:26:57.900 --> 00:26:59.467
and measure all those things.

00:26:59.467 --> 00:27:01.300
But when it comes to
cloud, for some reason,

00:27:01.300 --> 00:27:03.660
those economics are hard
to instill in people.

00:27:03.660 --> 00:27:06.780
But what I would say is
to do yourself justice,

00:27:06.780 --> 00:27:08.950
look at the software ROI factor.

00:27:08.950 --> 00:27:11.166
There's a lot of sort of--

00:27:11.166 --> 00:27:13.290
at this point, there's a
lot of data and collateral

00:27:13.290 --> 00:27:16.290
about just how much faster folks
can work in a cloud environment

00:27:16.290 --> 00:27:19.590
with open APIs, and open
source, et cetera than they

00:27:19.590 --> 00:27:21.210
can in a non-cloud environment.

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:23.992
Or what happens if a
developer can work twice as

00:27:23.992 --> 00:27:25.200
fast with an easier tool set?

00:27:28.154 --> 00:27:29.570
If you don't measure
that, I think

00:27:29.570 --> 00:27:32.315
it becomes hard to
see the cloud value.

00:27:32.315 --> 00:27:34.190
If you just start looking
at pure economics--

00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:36.824
economics are better, but they
just look a whole lot better

00:27:36.824 --> 00:27:38.240
when you make sure
that you factor

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:40.586
in exactly how much better
your teams are operating.

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:51.740
This is how I see things going
from a consumption perspective,

00:27:51.740 --> 00:27:54.800
is that if you think
about buying a server

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:58.580
and racking and stacking it,
you're akin to owning it.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:01.562
If you think about locking
into a one year agreement

00:28:01.562 --> 00:28:03.770
to use a particular VM for
a certain amount of time--

00:28:03.770 --> 00:28:05.060
[INAUDIBLE] or something
like that-- that's

00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:06.290
kind of akin to renting it.

00:28:06.290 --> 00:28:08.780
You're paying a fixed amount
for a large block of time.

00:28:08.780 --> 00:28:12.380
What's happening now-- and we
see this just in every single

00:28:12.380 --> 00:28:13.760
product--

00:28:13.760 --> 00:28:17.570
is that the grain is becoming
as small as it can be.

00:28:17.570 --> 00:28:19.310
So I talked about
our VM pricing.

00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:21.620
Our VM pricing is per minute.

00:28:21.620 --> 00:28:24.920
That's close to as close to
the grain as you can get,

00:28:24.920 --> 00:28:28.415
in terms of consuming that, but
per the gigabyte, per the API

00:28:28.415 --> 00:28:28.970
call.

00:28:28.970 --> 00:28:31.100
There's a number of
companies right now

00:28:31.100 --> 00:28:32.900
who have found a
whole new economy

00:28:32.900 --> 00:28:34.880
in externalizing their APIs.

00:28:34.880 --> 00:28:37.580
Google has recently acquired
a company called Apigee.

00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:39.620
And that was the entire
mode of that company

00:28:39.620 --> 00:28:41.330
was helping
companies externalize

00:28:41.330 --> 00:28:43.520
their APIs for some
sort of consumption,

00:28:43.520 --> 00:28:47.150
in many cases around
monetary consumption.

00:28:47.150 --> 00:28:50.110
And so, as an IT
leader or somebody

00:28:50.110 --> 00:28:53.330
who is trying to consume
services, what this should do

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:55.760
is it should make you ask
the question as you're

00:28:55.760 --> 00:28:56.750
consuming a service.

00:28:56.750 --> 00:28:58.517
Well, are we consuming
it, or does it

00:28:58.517 --> 00:29:01.100
look more like we're renting it,
or in fact, does it look more

00:29:01.100 --> 00:29:02.110
like we're buying
it, because we're

00:29:02.110 --> 00:29:04.193
going to be stuck with
this thing for three years?

00:29:04.193 --> 00:29:06.260
In a perfect world,
you could consume,

00:29:06.260 --> 00:29:07.790
which basically
means you just pay

00:29:07.790 --> 00:29:09.800
per the metered use
of that service,

00:29:09.800 --> 00:29:12.930
pretty much on the scale of
what the best pricing is.

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:15.890
That's the win-win arrangement.

00:29:15.890 --> 00:29:19.910
And so, as you're looking
to how you consume,

00:29:19.910 --> 00:29:24.170
or how you acquire IT services--
including a cloud environment

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:27.300
for each of the dimensions that
you're acquiring-- think about,

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:28.970
well, is there a
consumption-based model

00:29:28.970 --> 00:29:30.620
for what I'm doing
so that I don't

00:29:30.620 --> 00:29:32.161
have to get stuck
into something that

00:29:32.161 --> 00:29:33.764
looks like a lock-in
or a longer term?

00:29:33.764 --> 00:29:35.180
There are many of
the things we do

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:37.460
on top of Google Cloud that
actually help that consumption

00:29:37.460 --> 00:29:37.959
model.

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:46.077
There's an interesting trend.

00:29:46.077 --> 00:29:47.910
I'll mention it and
I'll highlight it later,

00:29:47.910 --> 00:29:52.330
but when a VM can spin
up in roughly a minute--

00:29:52.330 --> 00:29:55.620
that's about the time it
takes for our VMs to spin up,

00:29:55.620 --> 00:29:57.150
and we're charged per minute.

00:29:57.150 --> 00:29:59.160
It makes all kinds of patterns--

00:29:59.160 --> 00:30:01.162
all kinds of new
patterns possible.

00:30:01.162 --> 00:30:02.620
So if anybody here
has been-- who's

00:30:02.620 --> 00:30:04.127
here managed a
Hadoop cluster or has

00:30:04.127 --> 00:30:06.210
had to have a Hadoop cluster
in their environment?

00:30:06.210 --> 00:30:07.630
And so, you think about
managing a Hadoop cluster,

00:30:07.630 --> 00:30:10.213
and it's like this monolithic
ad server, ad server, ad server.

00:30:10.213 --> 00:30:12.900
And you're growing
this HDFS environment,

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:15.927
or you're doing it to
increase your worker

00:30:15.927 --> 00:30:17.510
capability, or your
mapper capability,

00:30:17.510 --> 00:30:20.280
or your storage capability.

00:30:20.280 --> 00:30:23.060
But if we have VMs that
can spin up in 90 seconds

00:30:23.060 --> 00:30:25.780
or spin up in 60
seconds, then we've

00:30:25.780 --> 00:30:27.030
turned that model on its head.

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:28.655
We have a great blog
post that came out

00:30:28.655 --> 00:30:31.590
that basically showed how
customers were, essentially,

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:34.030
saying, well, I have
got this batch of data

00:30:34.030 --> 00:30:35.280
and I need to run a job on it.

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:36.810
I actually spin up
a cluster to run

00:30:36.810 --> 00:30:39.146
against the data,
output a report,

00:30:39.146 --> 00:30:40.520
and then I tear
down the cluster.

00:30:40.520 --> 00:30:42.870
And because I'm
paying by the minute,

00:30:42.870 --> 00:30:44.850
that's a pretty easy
operation to accommodate.

00:30:44.850 --> 00:30:49.170
I'll talk about that
again in a second.

00:30:49.170 --> 00:30:54.870
Security-- this, by and large,
it becomes the sticky point,

00:30:54.870 --> 00:30:57.640
because this is where compliance
officer starts to get involved,

00:30:57.640 --> 00:31:00.930
and [INAUDIBLE] starts get
involved, and IT starts--

00:31:00.930 --> 00:31:01.860
just let it go.

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:02.910
Just let it go.

00:31:02.910 --> 00:31:05.010
We don't want you
to let anything go.

00:31:05.010 --> 00:31:09.120
There's a lot of
security framework

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:10.450
built into the platform.

00:31:10.450 --> 00:31:14.760
I think that not hitting this
head-on in your discussions--

00:31:14.760 --> 00:31:18.780
hit it head-on with Google, as
you engage with Google head-on.

00:31:18.780 --> 00:31:24.030
But it's absolutely something
you'll want to engage on.

00:31:24.030 --> 00:31:27.120
So right now at Google, there
are something like over 600

00:31:27.120 --> 00:31:29.617
dedicated security engineers.

00:31:29.617 --> 00:31:31.200
These are folks that
actually deal day

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:34.460
in and day out with security.

00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:37.480
That research link that
I sent in the beginning,

00:31:37.480 --> 00:31:39.120
that research link--
we have something

00:31:39.120 --> 00:31:41.970
like 160 publications
on just security things

00:31:41.970 --> 00:31:45.947
that have been adopted
by other companies.

00:31:45.947 --> 00:31:48.030
Companies have been formed
by some of the research

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:49.429
that we put out.

00:31:49.429 --> 00:31:50.970
But we've made steps
to, essentially,

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:54.140
make sure that you can
trust us but verify.

00:31:54.140 --> 00:31:56.640
The medium, the language
we use to disseminate

00:31:56.640 --> 00:31:58.260
how we adhere to a--

00:31:58.260 --> 00:32:01.620
how we adhere to a
certain security bar

00:32:01.620 --> 00:32:04.772
is by means of certifications
and attestations.

00:32:04.772 --> 00:32:06.980
And so, those are the
certifications and attestations

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:08.744
that we have.

00:32:08.744 --> 00:32:09.660
But that's not enough.

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:10.900
That's the trust but verify.

00:32:10.900 --> 00:32:13.060
It means that somebody else
has come into our environment,

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:15.240
verified everything we
had, and made it externally

00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:17.250
digestible by yourselves.

00:32:17.250 --> 00:32:19.260
We also have become
very prescriptive,

00:32:19.260 --> 00:32:21.689
and prescriptive in
the sense that we

00:32:21.689 --> 00:32:22.980
put out a security white paper.

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:25.021
And we tell you, essentially,
from an application

00:32:25.021 --> 00:32:28.725
perspective, how do you build
an application securely?

00:32:28.725 --> 00:32:31.350
These are white papers that are
available currently, right now.

00:32:34.170 --> 00:32:38.220
And the important thing
to realize there is it's,

00:32:38.220 --> 00:32:40.800
obviously, when we talk
about a secure platform,

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:43.200
we both have skin in the game.

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:45.690
Google is making sure that,
essentially, all the attack

00:32:45.690 --> 00:32:48.570
vectors that fall
within the providers--

00:32:48.570 --> 00:32:51.450
with us, as a cloud provider
spectrum, are taken care of.

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:54.060
But that doesn't stop you from
leaving SSH open to the world

00:32:54.060 --> 00:32:57.780
and putting your private
key in a public bucket.

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:00.810
If you don't believe
mean, just Google that.

00:33:00.810 --> 00:33:03.080
You'll see people do
things like that--

00:33:03.080 --> 00:33:05.280
or leaving a bucket wide
open and it's your backups.

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:11.010
So with great power
comes great stupidity.

00:33:11.010 --> 00:33:13.110
Obviously, we want to
make sure that you're

00:33:13.110 --> 00:33:15.080
using the right controls.

00:33:15.080 --> 00:33:17.550
But the underlying is there is--

00:33:17.550 --> 00:33:18.940
what is the inherent security.

00:33:18.940 --> 00:33:21.810
There's no reason for you
to question that or wonder

00:33:21.810 --> 00:33:22.590
if it exists.

00:33:22.590 --> 00:33:24.631
We say it's simply check
out the attestations.

00:33:24.631 --> 00:33:26.880
We can send you these, and
check out the white papers.

00:33:31.430 --> 00:33:36.320
How do I know Google's not
doing funny things with my data?

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:39.350
So, by default,
everything is encrypted.

00:33:39.350 --> 00:33:40.802
Everything is
encrypted in motion

00:33:40.802 --> 00:33:42.260
and everything's
encrypted at rest.

00:33:42.260 --> 00:33:44.424
This is the encryption at rest.

00:33:44.424 --> 00:33:46.340
There's a white paper
there at the bottom that

00:33:46.340 --> 00:33:48.830
goes into it in far more
detail, but nothing is ever

00:33:48.830 --> 00:33:52.920
written inside a Google
platform that's not encrypted.

00:33:52.920 --> 00:33:56.510
We do some unique things,
though, on top of that.

00:33:56.510 --> 00:34:00.799
One is you get to pick where you
want to be on this continuum.

00:34:00.799 --> 00:34:03.090
If you want it fully managed,
it means we just encrypt.

00:34:03.090 --> 00:34:05.907
There's nothing we write of
your data that's not encrypted.

00:34:05.907 --> 00:34:07.490
But we also have
another option, which

00:34:07.490 --> 00:34:10.400
is essentially you can
hold the keys of how

00:34:10.400 --> 00:34:12.230
your data is encrypted.

00:34:12.230 --> 00:34:13.550
That takes two flavors.

00:34:13.550 --> 00:34:16.340
That's a flavor where we manage
the apparatus, the cloud--

00:34:16.340 --> 00:34:18.560
essentially, the cloud
service that holds the keys,

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:19.489
but it's your--

00:34:19.489 --> 00:34:21.500
you dictate what
applications and what

00:34:21.500 --> 00:34:23.730
systems can retrieve keys out.

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:26.156
That's a service we
have called Cloud KMS.

00:34:26.156 --> 00:34:27.739
And we do something
unique here, which

00:34:27.739 --> 00:34:30.797
is customer supplied
encryption keys.

00:34:30.797 --> 00:34:33.380
And what this means is this is
your own key management system,

00:34:33.380 --> 00:34:35.713
where you can bring your keys
into Google's environment,

00:34:35.713 --> 00:34:39.350
and your data will be
encrypted with your keys.

00:34:39.350 --> 00:34:42.902
And, essentially, what
that means is that when--

00:34:42.902 --> 00:34:44.360
the key, as it's
transmitted to us,

00:34:44.360 --> 00:34:46.699
will be used for decryption
and encryption operations,

00:34:46.699 --> 00:34:48.469
but we never store
the key on disk.

00:34:48.469 --> 00:34:53.330
It's only used in memory, and
you supply it in operations

00:34:53.330 --> 00:34:56.360
that you need to use
encrypted data on.

00:34:56.360 --> 00:35:00.700
And that applies currently to
our VMs and our cloud storage.

00:35:00.700 --> 00:35:02.630
The important
thing to realize is

00:35:02.630 --> 00:35:04.430
when you're at
the fully managed,

00:35:04.430 --> 00:35:06.767
there's sort of an
efficiency-- or I

00:35:06.767 --> 00:35:08.600
would say there's a
trade-off of efficiency.

00:35:08.600 --> 00:35:11.130
As you move towards the customer
supplied keys, obviously,

00:35:11.130 --> 00:35:13.700
there's-- it implies a higher
number of operations that are

00:35:13.700 --> 00:35:15.890
going to be having to take
place between a customer

00:35:15.890 --> 00:35:16.723
and the environment.

00:35:16.723 --> 00:35:18.775
So if you're at the
fully managed side that's

00:35:18.775 --> 00:35:20.150
native to the
platform, your data

00:35:20.150 --> 00:35:21.620
is always written and encrypted.

00:35:21.620 --> 00:35:23.990
If you're on the other
side of the spectrum,

00:35:23.990 --> 00:35:25.910
then there's a higher
number of operations

00:35:25.910 --> 00:35:27.710
for these decrypt
things that take on.

00:35:27.710 --> 00:35:29.126
But the important
thing is you can

00:35:29.126 --> 00:35:31.550
feel very comfortable that
your data is never unencrypted.

00:35:31.550 --> 00:35:33.300
And if you ever said
I wanted-- like, hey,

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:35.250
this is really,
really sensitive data.

00:35:35.250 --> 00:35:37.850
I never want there to be
a possibility of looking

00:35:37.850 --> 00:35:39.262
at it without my involvement.

00:35:39.262 --> 00:35:41.470
In fact, I don't even want
the keys inside of Google.

00:35:41.470 --> 00:35:42.830
We give you that flexibility.

00:35:42.830 --> 00:35:44.829
I think that's something
unique to us right now.

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:52.400
Similarly, there's never
a mystery of who did what.

00:35:52.400 --> 00:35:54.975
I think this is a
fear or phobia of who

00:35:54.975 --> 00:35:57.860
opened my environment, who
opened my firewall, who

00:35:57.860 --> 00:35:58.891
exported my data.

00:35:58.891 --> 00:36:00.640
All that stuff is
available in audit logs.

00:36:00.640 --> 00:36:02.120
This is pretty standard stuff.

00:36:02.120 --> 00:36:06.410
This is available to you
to see on our audit logs.

00:36:06.410 --> 00:36:11.000
It's available for you to stream
out and feed it into your sim

00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:13.220
system, to feed it
to Splunk, to feed

00:36:13.220 --> 00:36:15.982
it any number of
downstream systems.

00:36:15.982 --> 00:36:17.690
So we don't make it
a mystery as to who's

00:36:17.690 --> 00:36:20.470
done what on your environment.

00:36:20.470 --> 00:36:23.450
This is a fun one.

00:36:23.450 --> 00:36:27.230
Security keys-- how many
people have used security keys?

00:36:27.230 --> 00:36:27.984
Cool.

00:36:27.984 --> 00:36:29.400
Do all you people
work for Google?

00:36:29.400 --> 00:36:30.830
No?

00:36:30.830 --> 00:36:33.200
So this is really
something I found fun.

00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:35.930
I joined Google, and I was
told, hey, we don't have VPN.

00:36:35.930 --> 00:36:38.160
I was like, yes, no
more working from home.

00:36:38.160 --> 00:36:39.951
And they're like, that's
not what it means.

00:36:39.951 --> 00:36:41.660
We just don't have
VPN within Google.

00:36:41.660 --> 00:36:43.760
We use different
kind of mechanisms.

00:36:43.760 --> 00:36:45.170
We assume.

00:36:45.170 --> 00:36:47.314
We don't make any
assumptions about open trust,

00:36:47.314 --> 00:36:48.980
and open networks,
and things like that.

00:36:48.980 --> 00:36:52.770
We don't have this hard,
outer candy shell model.

00:36:52.770 --> 00:36:56.210
We essentially assert identity
and encryption and security

00:36:56.210 --> 00:36:59.181
at every single interaction
and every single call.

00:36:59.181 --> 00:37:00.180
And this is one of them.

00:37:00.180 --> 00:37:01.772
So it hinges on identity.

00:37:01.772 --> 00:37:03.380
Identity is key.

00:37:03.380 --> 00:37:05.720
Identity is, essentially,
at a fundamental level,

00:37:05.720 --> 00:37:10.120
how you endorse somebody
within your environment

00:37:10.120 --> 00:37:12.800
to do something inside of
the Google Cloud environment.

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:15.410
And so, we basically put
identity on a pedestal.

00:37:15.410 --> 00:37:18.020
It's very important to us for
that not to get screwed up.

00:37:18.830 --> 00:37:21.644
So Google came up with
a standard called UTF.

00:37:21.644 --> 00:37:23.060
That's also on the
research paper,

00:37:23.060 --> 00:37:24.310
and we've externalized it now.

00:37:24.310 --> 00:37:26.920
It's taken up by something
called the FIDO Alliance.

00:37:26.920 --> 00:37:29.480
And so, these keys
are made by folks

00:37:29.480 --> 00:37:30.860
that implement that standard.

00:37:30.860 --> 00:37:33.219
But this is unphishable--

00:37:33.219 --> 00:37:34.010
phishing resistant.

00:37:34.010 --> 00:37:35.640
I apologize--
phishing resistant.

00:37:35.640 --> 00:37:40.070
This is your best bet for
avoiding network-based vector

00:37:40.070 --> 00:37:40.700
attacks.

00:37:40.700 --> 00:37:42.650
So what that means is if
you have a laptop right now

00:37:42.650 --> 00:37:44.090
and somebody is looking
over your shoulder,

00:37:44.090 --> 00:37:46.173
and they've discovered
your username and password,

00:37:46.173 --> 00:37:48.519
and they take your
laptop, and it has a key,

00:37:48.519 --> 00:37:49.310
you're out of luck.

00:37:49.310 --> 00:37:50.420
That's bad.

00:37:50.420 --> 00:37:53.420
But somebody in the middle--
this is the more prevalent case

00:37:53.420 --> 00:37:54.590
is you're being phished.

00:37:54.590 --> 00:37:57.500
Somebody sends you a link,
an email that looks legit.

00:37:57.500 --> 00:37:58.585
You click on it.

00:37:58.585 --> 00:37:59.960
It's very
sophisticated nowadays,

00:37:59.960 --> 00:38:01.970
and you're duped into
giving away your username

00:38:01.970 --> 00:38:04.160
and password, heaven forbid.

00:38:04.160 --> 00:38:07.412
So when an attacker
has that, they're

00:38:07.412 --> 00:38:09.620
going to turn around and
try to use those credentials

00:38:09.620 --> 00:38:11.230
immediately on
whatever source system

00:38:11.230 --> 00:38:12.507
they fooled you into using.

00:38:12.507 --> 00:38:14.090
And so, folks said,
OK, you know what?

00:38:14.090 --> 00:38:16.010
We'll do this one-time
password thing

00:38:16.010 --> 00:38:19.880
where we'll send a code to your
phone, or a six digit rotating

00:38:19.880 --> 00:38:24.356
code, or a one-time password,
and that will protect you.

00:38:24.356 --> 00:38:26.480
If somebody's fooled you
into sending your username

00:38:26.480 --> 00:38:29.330
and password, they're fooling
you into sending that, too.

00:38:29.330 --> 00:38:32.270
So the idea is that this--

00:38:32.270 --> 00:38:35.210
because you are in a position
to give away those credentials.

00:38:35.210 --> 00:38:37.587
You can say, oh, it's one,
two, three, four, five, six.

00:38:37.587 --> 00:38:39.170
Here, one, two,
three, four, five six.

00:38:39.170 --> 00:38:40.020
Thank you very much.

00:38:40.020 --> 00:38:42.460
I'll turn around and
break into your account.

00:38:42.460 --> 00:38:46.160
This is outside your scope
of knowing what's going on.

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:47.909
These keys get
enrolled in Google.

00:38:47.909 --> 00:38:50.450
And, essentially, for those of
you who are familiar with SSH,

00:38:50.450 --> 00:38:52.283
this is equivalent to
what happens with SSH.

00:38:52.283 --> 00:38:54.730
There is two halves
of a key pair

00:38:54.730 --> 00:38:56.900
that no human can
cryptographically

00:38:56.900 --> 00:38:58.220
get in the middle of it.

00:38:58.220 --> 00:39:00.860
So if you get an assertion
back from whatever

00:39:00.860 --> 00:39:03.890
website and your
security goes, that

00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:05.832
doesn't match my
signature, then you

00:39:05.832 --> 00:39:08.290
know that basically you're not
talking to the source you've

00:39:08.290 --> 00:39:09.260
enrolled it to.

00:39:09.260 --> 00:39:12.200
Conversely, if somebody tries
to break into your account,

00:39:12.200 --> 00:39:13.380
because they have your
user name and password,

00:39:13.380 --> 00:39:14.420
and they just somehow
figure out they're

00:39:14.420 --> 00:39:16.003
going to spoof this
and send something

00:39:16.003 --> 00:39:16.962
random, the same thing.

00:39:16.962 --> 00:39:19.377
They're going to be like, that
doesn't match the challenge

00:39:19.377 --> 00:39:20.640
phrase of what was enrolled.

00:39:20.640 --> 00:39:23.900
So we found this to be, again,
one of the most effective ways.

00:39:23.900 --> 00:39:27.207
And the other thing is, we found
that it takes how many seconds

00:39:27.207 --> 00:39:29.540
to break out the one-time
password, log into your phone,

00:39:29.540 --> 00:39:31.880
extract those keys,
and send them over.

00:39:31.880 --> 00:39:33.229
You just touch this.

00:39:33.229 --> 00:39:34.062
That's all it takes.

00:39:34.062 --> 00:39:36.230
It takes a human touch,
because if it didn't require

00:39:36.230 --> 00:39:38.210
a human intervention,
then you can essentially

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:40.640
write something browser-based
that could spoof a touch.

00:39:40.640 --> 00:39:42.842
So this requires a
physical human touch.

00:39:42.842 --> 00:39:43.550
It's really cool.

00:39:43.550 --> 00:39:45.466
That's why I spent a
little bit of time on it.

00:39:45.466 --> 00:39:49.789
I think even if, for
nothing else, spend the $10

00:39:49.789 --> 00:39:50.330
and get this.

00:39:53.490 --> 00:39:55.605
On to networking and operations.

00:39:58.720 --> 00:40:01.635
Identity-- so piggybacking
on how critical identity is

00:40:01.635 --> 00:40:04.010
to get right, and making sure
nobody breaks the identity,

00:40:04.010 --> 00:40:07.930
and nobody falsely claims
who they are, in order

00:40:07.930 --> 00:40:10.260
to do anything inside of the
Google Cloud environment,

00:40:10.260 --> 00:40:12.360
we need to assert your identity.

00:40:12.360 --> 00:40:14.267
And so, we need to
know who you are.

00:40:14.267 --> 00:40:16.100
And so, we have a couple
of ways to do that.

00:40:16.100 --> 00:40:18.558
So a big tip here that I've
heard from a lot of customers--

00:40:18.558 --> 00:40:20.300
and we've made
this pretty easy--

00:40:20.300 --> 00:40:23.300
is use something called
our directory sync,

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:26.420
which can take your
directory and replicate that

00:40:26.420 --> 00:40:28.790
into your G Suite instance.

00:40:28.790 --> 00:40:31.340
And so, that way we know
about users, and groups,

00:40:31.340 --> 00:40:32.540
and attributes.

00:40:32.540 --> 00:40:34.280
Passwords don't get synced.

00:40:34.280 --> 00:40:37.610
Passwords you still have to
create at a Google level.

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:40.187
But this way, if somebody
leaves your environment,

00:40:40.187 --> 00:40:42.020
directory sync will
trigger and remove them.

00:40:42.020 --> 00:40:45.510
If you add somebody to a group,
those groups can be used.

00:40:45.510 --> 00:40:47.880
We can do SSL back to
your-- we, essentially,

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:50.450
say I'm going to federate back
to some other authentication

00:40:50.450 --> 00:40:52.910
system, whether using a
third party hosted version

00:40:52.910 --> 00:40:54.657
or on prem.

00:40:54.657 --> 00:40:56.990
So you can say, OK, when
somebody needs to authenticate,

00:40:56.990 --> 00:40:58.740
I want that to come
all the way back home.

00:40:58.740 --> 00:41:00.830
I don't want that to
be inside of Google--

00:41:00.830 --> 00:41:02.849
or either way.

00:41:02.849 --> 00:41:04.640
We also give you
something is that once you

00:41:04.640 --> 00:41:08.492
have authenticated within
the browser experience

00:41:08.492 --> 00:41:10.700
when you log into Google
Cloud, we give you something

00:41:10.700 --> 00:41:13.160
called Google Cloud Shell.

00:41:13.160 --> 00:41:17.625
And so, it's essentially a
browser-based SSH experience

00:41:17.625 --> 00:41:20.000
that once you've established
your identity and you assert

00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:21.920
that I'm so-and-so,
and you've done your--

00:41:21.920 --> 00:41:24.052
if you have it,
your security key,

00:41:24.052 --> 00:41:25.510
you don't have to
rely on something

00:41:25.510 --> 00:41:28.330
as static as opening a port
and opening an IP address.

00:41:28.330 --> 00:41:30.580
We can, essentially, say now
that we know who you are,

00:41:30.580 --> 00:41:32.110
we'll give you a
console that will

00:41:32.110 --> 00:41:34.810
help you drive your interactions
with the cloud environment,

00:41:34.810 --> 00:41:38.100
rather than having to set
up all your tools on prem

00:41:38.100 --> 00:41:39.460
and figure that out.

00:41:39.460 --> 00:41:40.774
So it's called Cloud Shell.

00:41:40.774 --> 00:41:41.940
I'll let you check that out.

00:41:45.340 --> 00:41:49.090
This is how we organize
your cloud environment.

00:41:49.090 --> 00:41:51.890
The top level are something
called an organization node.

00:41:51.890 --> 00:41:55.252
So within, say, a lot
of alternate providers,

00:41:55.252 --> 00:41:57.460
it's very easy to lose track
of what accounts I have.

00:41:57.460 --> 00:41:58.810
That's a big concern
that I've heard

00:41:58.810 --> 00:42:00.810
from a number of customers
is like, hey, I don't

00:42:00.810 --> 00:42:01.930
want to have cloud sprawl.

00:42:01.930 --> 00:42:04.372
I don't want every group
to have their own account,

00:42:04.372 --> 00:42:06.580
and they're spinning up how
many different resources,

00:42:06.580 --> 00:42:07.990
and their billing is
getting out of control,

00:42:07.990 --> 00:42:09.240
and I'm going to lose control.

00:42:09.240 --> 00:42:12.130
As an IT advocate, everything
eventually falls on me.

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:14.820
So an organization node allows
you to have that top level

00:42:14.820 --> 00:42:17.680
visibility of all
the projects that

00:42:17.680 --> 00:42:19.247
are below that organization.

00:42:19.247 --> 00:42:20.830
So you never have
to feel like, hey, I

00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:22.996
don't know what's going on
inside of my environment,

00:42:22.996 --> 00:42:25.540
and who's doing what,
who has access to what.

00:42:25.540 --> 00:42:26.980
And this applies
to policies, too.

00:42:26.980 --> 00:42:29.710
So you can apply a policy
at the organization node

00:42:29.710 --> 00:42:32.320
and have it trickle down.

00:42:32.320 --> 00:42:35.410
And it'll trickle down,
essentially, to projects.

00:42:35.410 --> 00:42:40.610
Projects are essentially
containers for everything

00:42:40.610 --> 00:42:42.820
that represents anything
under that project.

00:42:42.820 --> 00:42:45.740
And so, you can think of
it like, hey, we want--

00:42:45.740 --> 00:42:48.620
you could do it by dev test,
QA, integrity, production,

00:42:48.620 --> 00:42:51.280
et cetera, or you can
do it by application.

00:42:51.280 --> 00:42:53.364
CRM is in this project
or so on and so forth.

00:42:53.364 --> 00:42:55.030
And a lot of times
it's a mix and match.

00:42:55.030 --> 00:43:01.750
You have projects by purpose
and projects by function.

00:43:01.750 --> 00:43:05.060
But you can do some other
things with projects as well.

00:43:05.060 --> 00:43:07.511
You can install
SSH keys centrally.

00:43:07.511 --> 00:43:09.010
So if you're like,
hey, this project

00:43:09.010 --> 00:43:10.780
is going to be for
this development effort

00:43:10.780 --> 00:43:12.700
and these developers
need access to it,

00:43:12.700 --> 00:43:15.880
you can put in everybody's
SSH key into that public key

00:43:15.880 --> 00:43:17.920
into that project and
it'll be propagated

00:43:17.920 --> 00:43:22.450
through into the environment
for anybody to use those VMs.

00:43:22.450 --> 00:43:24.752
You invite people to projects,
which is kind of cool.

00:43:24.752 --> 00:43:26.710
So you don't have to
statically provision them.

00:43:26.710 --> 00:43:28.793
You can literally say I
want to invite this person

00:43:28.793 --> 00:43:29.797
by their email address.

00:43:29.797 --> 00:43:31.630
They're associated with
the billing account.

00:43:34.720 --> 00:43:36.220
So you can have
multiple accounts--

00:43:36.220 --> 00:43:38.469
multiple projects associated
with the billing account.

00:43:38.469 --> 00:43:42.760
And so, I get asked how would
you recommend straightening out

00:43:42.760 --> 00:43:43.570
projects?

00:43:43.570 --> 00:43:45.490
And I say do both.

00:43:45.490 --> 00:43:48.300
Have projects that are based on
our environment and function--

00:43:48.300 --> 00:43:52.340
like, again, CRM or customer
service, dev test, broad QA,

00:43:52.340 --> 00:43:53.660
et cetera.

00:43:53.660 --> 00:43:54.910
But give developers a project.

00:43:54.910 --> 00:43:57.180
Projects don't cost anything.

00:43:57.180 --> 00:43:59.540
And so, you can have a bunch
of projects all associated

00:43:59.540 --> 00:44:00.790
with the same billing account.

00:44:00.790 --> 00:44:03.989
Nothing ramps up cloud know-how.

00:44:03.989 --> 00:44:06.280
If you think back to my
first-- one of the first slides

00:44:06.280 --> 00:44:08.292
was [INAUDIBLE] how
do I accelerate this?

00:44:08.292 --> 00:44:10.000
What are the skills
that I need for this?

00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:12.315
Give everybody a sandbox.

00:44:12.315 --> 00:44:13.690
You can set billing
alarms so you

00:44:13.690 --> 00:44:16.630
know when somebody is getting
crazy, but give them a sandbox.

00:44:16.630 --> 00:44:20.350
Give them a target in
terms of how quickly you

00:44:20.350 --> 00:44:21.940
want something turned over.

00:44:21.940 --> 00:44:23.500
There was something
a while ago that

00:44:23.500 --> 00:44:24.958
started to go out
of fashion, which

00:44:24.958 --> 00:44:28.620
was internal hack-a-thons, and
challenging your developers

00:44:28.620 --> 00:44:31.300
and your IT folks
to deliver something

00:44:31.300 --> 00:44:33.700
within a certain
timed box activity.

00:44:33.700 --> 00:44:35.620
So what I found
accelerates the best

00:44:35.620 --> 00:44:37.960
is when you give every
developer their own project.

00:44:37.960 --> 00:44:39.040
At the end of the day,
if they don't use it,

00:44:39.040 --> 00:44:40.040
it's just sitting there.

00:44:40.040 --> 00:44:42.830
It doesn't cost you anything.

00:44:42.830 --> 00:44:49.180
But that's what I've seen
help accelerate cloud usage.

00:44:49.180 --> 00:44:51.529
And the developer projects,
keep those isolated.

00:44:51.529 --> 00:44:54.070
So folks are like, oh, I don't
know what they're going to do.

00:44:54.070 --> 00:44:55.360
Are they going to mess around?

00:44:55.360 --> 00:44:57.486
There's more risk to some
developer going rogue

00:44:57.486 --> 00:44:59.110
within the environment
and accidentally

00:44:59.110 --> 00:45:01.697
running a DHCP server
or some nefarious server

00:45:01.697 --> 00:45:03.530
without knowing it,
inside your environment.

00:45:03.530 --> 00:45:05.976
And that has a huge
blast radius impact.

00:45:05.976 --> 00:45:08.350
When you create a project from
a Google Cloud perspective

00:45:08.350 --> 00:45:10.780
and you give every
developer their own project,

00:45:10.780 --> 00:45:14.739
well, if they go haywire
and do something crazy,

00:45:14.739 --> 00:45:16.780
it's not connected back
to your home environment.

00:45:16.780 --> 00:45:21.490
So it's a nice,
safe way to instill

00:45:21.490 --> 00:45:26.850
or to instigate moving forward.

00:45:26.850 --> 00:45:30.580
Networking-- so
moving down the list.

00:45:30.580 --> 00:45:33.560
This is one where if
you don't get it right,

00:45:33.560 --> 00:45:36.304
it can be so painful, because
people have deployed VMs,

00:45:36.304 --> 00:45:37.720
and they've deployed
applications.

00:45:37.720 --> 00:45:39.761
And then you take it to
networking and networking

00:45:39.761 --> 00:45:42.032
is like, thank you
for using 10.1.1.1.

00:45:42.032 --> 00:45:43.240
That's what our company uses.

00:45:43.240 --> 00:45:45.240
You can't use this, and
you have this conflict.

00:45:45.240 --> 00:45:47.899
And how do you deal with it?

00:45:47.899 --> 00:45:50.440
So a couple of ways to deal with
it-- one, involve networking

00:45:50.440 --> 00:45:53.780
early, but two, start
with automation.

00:45:53.780 --> 00:45:55.540
Try and not, as
much as possible,

00:45:55.540 --> 00:45:57.760
to craft things by hand.

00:45:57.760 --> 00:46:00.760
Automate using whatever
popular tool suite

00:46:00.760 --> 00:46:04.010
you have, but automate
this from the beginning.

00:46:04.010 --> 00:46:06.066
So I only mentioned
networking on its own,

00:46:06.066 --> 00:46:08.440
because this is where I see
a lot of folks when it starts

00:46:08.440 --> 00:46:10.315
to go to production, and they
get the network folks involved,

00:46:10.315 --> 00:46:11.977
and they want to
talk about firewalls.

00:46:11.977 --> 00:46:13.560
The first reaction
is like, oh, you've

00:46:13.560 --> 00:46:15.280
totally clobbered our
internal IP space.

00:46:15.280 --> 00:46:17.440
You can't launch with this.

00:46:17.440 --> 00:46:19.960
So some things here to realize
is that, if you notice,

00:46:19.960 --> 00:46:21.070
we have different
regions, et cetera.

00:46:21.070 --> 00:46:22.490
I think that's pretty familiar.

00:46:22.490 --> 00:46:24.650
We have different zones.

00:46:24.650 --> 00:46:33.929
But if you notice the subnets,
they go between regions--

00:46:33.929 --> 00:46:35.470
or you go between
this whole network.

00:46:35.470 --> 00:46:37.520
And you notice that you
have subnets in one region

00:46:37.520 --> 00:46:38.485
and subnets in another region.

00:46:38.485 --> 00:46:40.693
And it looks like there's
a backbone connecting them.

00:46:40.693 --> 00:46:41.890
That's because there is.

00:46:41.890 --> 00:46:47.170
So you can have a subnetwork
that spans zones, and you

00:46:47.170 --> 00:46:50.680
can have a subnetwork that looks
in a totally different region.

00:46:50.680 --> 00:46:52.390
And it's like as if
you, as a company,

00:46:52.390 --> 00:46:54.257
created a data center
in one, geography

00:46:54.257 --> 00:46:55.840
in a data center in
another, geography

00:46:55.840 --> 00:46:57.104
are a different continent.

00:46:57.104 --> 00:46:59.770
And you're like, hey, if we were
to create our own data centers,

00:46:59.770 --> 00:47:02.440
we'd put some VPN,
or MPLS, et cetera.

00:47:02.440 --> 00:47:04.940
And we'd expect native
connectivity between VMs in one

00:47:04.940 --> 00:47:06.062
and VMs in the other.

00:47:06.062 --> 00:47:07.270
That's given to you natively.

00:47:07.270 --> 00:47:10.490
That's on Google's
backbone, Google's network.

00:47:10.490 --> 00:47:13.390
You have private addressing
with no VPN end-to-end

00:47:13.390 --> 00:47:15.550
between all your
different regions

00:47:15.550 --> 00:47:19.730
and in the same project
in the same network.

00:47:23.581 --> 00:47:25.830
And that's probably different
than what we're used to.

00:47:25.830 --> 00:47:27.150
And so, what is that
global backbone?

00:47:27.150 --> 00:47:28.080
What is that global scope?

00:47:28.080 --> 00:47:29.454
I mean, this is
a current picture

00:47:29.454 --> 00:47:31.500
of current and
committed regions,

00:47:31.500 --> 00:47:33.190
points of presence, et cetera.

00:47:33.190 --> 00:47:34.890
But that's a nice
feature, is you're

00:47:34.890 --> 00:47:36.990
taking our fiber under
the ocean when you-- let's

00:47:36.990 --> 00:47:41.990
say you had an Asia deployment,
and a European deployment,

00:47:41.990 --> 00:47:43.549
and a US deployment.

00:47:43.549 --> 00:47:45.340
All that's riding across
a private network.

00:47:49.799 --> 00:47:51.840
Moving, again, onto the
DevOps side of the house,

00:47:51.840 --> 00:47:54.300
I just talked a minute
ago about automation.

00:47:54.300 --> 00:47:56.880
Yes, containers are a thing.

00:47:56.880 --> 00:47:57.820
What do they solve?

00:47:57.820 --> 00:48:00.356
They solve packaging.

00:48:00.356 --> 00:48:01.480
Containers solve packaging.

00:48:01.480 --> 00:48:03.840
They solve, basically, the
ability to bundle things

00:48:03.840 --> 00:48:05.820
up in a consistent way,
that's consistently

00:48:05.820 --> 00:48:07.980
executable in a
different environment,

00:48:07.980 --> 00:48:11.050
no matter how they're
deployed onto a host.

00:48:11.050 --> 00:48:15.120
So check positive
for awesomeness.

00:48:15.120 --> 00:48:17.880
Google has-- from a Linux
kernel perspective--

00:48:17.880 --> 00:48:19.620
Google's contributed
a number of things

00:48:19.620 --> 00:48:23.610
to Linux kernel that's
made containers possible.

00:48:23.610 --> 00:48:26.550
But from Google's own lessons--
in terms of how they run

00:48:26.550 --> 00:48:30.980
packages and internally on
something we call board--

00:48:30.980 --> 00:48:32.730
containers are just
one side of the story.

00:48:32.730 --> 00:48:34.560
How you orchestrate
and deploy containers

00:48:34.560 --> 00:48:35.880
is a whole different ballgame.

00:48:35.880 --> 00:48:37.590
How do you schedule
jobs, and how do you

00:48:37.590 --> 00:48:40.214
ensure that jobs have continuity
when they've been interrupted,

00:48:40.214 --> 00:48:41.340
et cetera?

00:48:41.340 --> 00:48:43.980
That's where things
get interesting.

00:48:43.980 --> 00:48:47.588
So there's a number of talks
today about Kubernetes--

00:48:47.588 --> 00:48:50.714
or over the next three days
about Kubernetes and the Google

00:48:50.714 --> 00:48:52.900
Container Engine that I
encourage you to talk to--

00:48:52.900 --> 00:48:57.720
or, I'm sorry, go attend there
and talk to the presenters

00:48:57.720 --> 00:48:59.460
there.

00:48:59.460 --> 00:49:03.420
But all of Google's
experience, what

00:49:03.420 --> 00:49:06.290
they've learned out of Borg
has been put into Kubernetes.

00:49:06.290 --> 00:49:08.590
And it's an open sourced
project in the wild.

00:49:08.590 --> 00:49:13.725
So one thing I'd mention
there, I talked a second ago

00:49:13.725 --> 00:49:14.600
about the networking.

00:49:14.600 --> 00:49:17.250
And it's like, hey, the best way
to ensure that you don't shoot

00:49:17.250 --> 00:49:19.860
yourself in the foot when
it comes to networking

00:49:19.860 --> 00:49:22.390
is to automate from the start.

00:49:22.390 --> 00:49:26.130
And if you had to choose between
automating or the new hotness--

00:49:26.130 --> 00:49:28.320
which is what containers are--

00:49:28.320 --> 00:49:29.280
choose automation.

00:49:29.280 --> 00:49:32.580
You'll get a lot more
bang for your buck

00:49:32.580 --> 00:49:34.710
about how to automate
different processes more

00:49:34.710 --> 00:49:36.162
so than containers.

00:49:36.162 --> 00:49:37.620
Once you've figured
out automation,

00:49:37.620 --> 00:49:40.590
absolutely, go for
containers, go for Kubernetes,

00:49:40.590 --> 00:49:42.780
but don't go the
other way around.

00:49:42.780 --> 00:49:45.395
Try as much as you can not to
build things by hand or deploy

00:49:45.395 --> 00:49:46.020
things by hand.

00:49:50.350 --> 00:49:52.542
The other thing about
containers that's interesting

00:49:52.542 --> 00:49:55.000
is that you may find yourself
challenging conventional ways

00:49:55.000 --> 00:49:55.510
to do things.

00:49:55.510 --> 00:49:57.551
If you think about
conventional host deployments,

00:49:57.551 --> 00:49:59.320
you deploy something.

00:49:59.320 --> 00:50:01.730
And in a traditional
environment you

00:50:01.730 --> 00:50:05.110
may, hey, let's
prime down anti-virus

00:50:05.110 --> 00:50:10.840
and all these bolt
on tools that you

00:50:10.840 --> 00:50:12.460
do, because of the
mentality of this

00:50:12.460 --> 00:50:14.560
being an everlasting
infrastructure that could

00:50:14.560 --> 00:50:16.511
get compromised at any time.

00:50:16.511 --> 00:50:18.010
Container sort of
changes that model

00:50:18.010 --> 00:50:22.210
or puts it on its head where
you would say, OK, well,

00:50:22.210 --> 00:50:24.640
I need to do things like
vulnerability scanning

00:50:24.640 --> 00:50:29.890
and security measurement as
part of my build process,

00:50:29.890 --> 00:50:32.530
because I'm creating
immutable images.

00:50:32.530 --> 00:50:34.180
And so, it doesn't really--

00:50:34.180 --> 00:50:35.920
there are new
techniques, in terms

00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:40.810
of probes and things
like that, to discover

00:50:40.810 --> 00:50:42.890
when your environment
is going haywire.

00:50:42.890 --> 00:50:44.950
But challenge the
traditional way

00:50:44.950 --> 00:50:48.040
that you may be dealing with
enterprise environments, where

00:50:48.040 --> 00:50:52.060
you lay down anti-virus and you
lay down all sorts of tools.

00:50:52.060 --> 00:50:53.710
Instead, you can
say, we've made sure

00:50:53.710 --> 00:50:55.620
that this immutable
artifact, this container

00:50:55.620 --> 00:50:57.782
is secure by part of
our build process,

00:50:57.782 --> 00:50:58.990
and so, the output is secure.

00:50:58.990 --> 00:51:00.531
Now, if something
happens, you simply

00:51:00.531 --> 00:51:02.461
spin out a new container.

00:51:06.150 --> 00:51:10.620
Don't over-engineer
the platform.

00:51:10.620 --> 00:51:11.880
An example of this is--

00:51:11.880 --> 00:51:13.950
our discs are already
heavily replicated

00:51:13.950 --> 00:51:15.840
in HA behind the scenes.

00:51:15.840 --> 00:51:19.100
So you don't need to create a
VM and then create two discs,

00:51:19.100 --> 00:51:21.360
and say, well, I've got to
RAID these two together,

00:51:21.360 --> 00:51:22.402
and then event one fails.

00:51:22.402 --> 00:51:24.776
We're already taking care of
all that replication for you

00:51:24.776 --> 00:51:25.736
behind the scenes.

00:51:25.736 --> 00:51:28.110
Similarly, if you're using
Google Cloud Storage or object

00:51:28.110 --> 00:51:30.400
storage, we're already making
a number of copies of that.

00:51:30.400 --> 00:51:32.080
So you don't have to say,
well, let me put one copy here.

00:51:32.080 --> 00:51:34.350
Let me create another
bucket called backup copy.

00:51:34.350 --> 00:51:37.931
That's already handled
for you behind the scenes.

00:51:37.931 --> 00:51:39.930
Similarly, we have a
product called Stackdriver,

00:51:39.930 --> 00:51:41.160
which facilitates logging.

00:51:41.160 --> 00:51:43.950
So if you're going to have an
entire logging infrastructure,

00:51:43.950 --> 00:51:46.170
realize there's actually a very
simple to use API that's part

00:51:46.170 --> 00:51:48.210
and parcel to every part
of your-- every project,

00:51:48.210 --> 00:51:49.550
that you can simply
just say send

00:51:49.550 --> 00:51:51.425
my logging into Stackdriver
and you get that.

00:51:54.610 --> 00:51:56.110
And there's a bunch
of other things.

00:51:56.110 --> 00:51:57.590
There's a built-in load balance.

00:51:57.590 --> 00:51:58.740
There's load balancers.

00:51:58.740 --> 00:52:00.210
There's tracing.

00:52:00.210 --> 00:52:03.630
You can actually do code
tracing, instance mitigation.

00:52:03.630 --> 00:52:05.520
There's things that
we provide in terms

00:52:05.520 --> 00:52:06.757
that we call live migration.

00:52:06.757 --> 00:52:08.340
So if you're inside
of a zone and a VM

00:52:08.340 --> 00:52:10.890
needs to be taken
down for maintenance,

00:52:10.890 --> 00:52:14.730
we live migrate that
VM to another VM

00:52:14.730 --> 00:52:16.950
so that you don't suffer
the consequence of us

00:52:16.950 --> 00:52:20.010
having to do maintenance.

00:52:20.010 --> 00:52:22.500
Similarly, if you're running--

00:52:22.500 --> 00:52:25.255
if you have data that's stored
on AWS, on S3, and you're like,

00:52:25.255 --> 00:52:26.880
hey, I'd like to use
Google as a backup

00:52:26.880 --> 00:52:29.476
or I'd like to use some of the
data analytics tool on Google,

00:52:29.476 --> 00:52:30.600
we have a transfer service.

00:52:30.600 --> 00:52:32.040
You don't have to write
your own transfer service

00:52:32.040 --> 00:52:33.030
or run a MapReduce job.

00:52:33.030 --> 00:52:35.071
You can literally go inside
of our object storage

00:52:35.071 --> 00:52:37.734
and say transfer service,
and then move that data over.

00:52:37.734 --> 00:52:39.150
And I have a lot
of customers that

00:52:39.150 --> 00:52:41.770
are doing that on
a regular basis,

00:52:41.770 --> 00:52:45.750
because they use Google
for data processing.

00:52:45.750 --> 00:52:49.680
This is a huge, huge advantage
that a number of customers

00:52:49.680 --> 00:52:50.460
have mentioned.

00:52:50.460 --> 00:52:52.350
This has really changed
the way they approach

00:52:52.350 --> 00:52:54.420
solving data science problems.

00:52:54.420 --> 00:52:57.600
Going back to the Hadoop
discussion earlier,

00:52:57.600 --> 00:52:59.160
you tend to have
a Hadoop cluster.

00:52:59.160 --> 00:53:01.159
And what I've heard from
a number of customers--

00:53:01.159 --> 00:53:02.610
specifically in
[? adtech-- ?] is

00:53:02.610 --> 00:53:04.318
that Hadoop cluster
starts to get really,

00:53:04.318 --> 00:53:06.890
really bogged down
with recurring jobs.

00:53:06.890 --> 00:53:09.750
Essentially, you have to figure
out how to batch jobs together.

00:53:09.750 --> 00:53:12.810
It doesn't really encourage
a lot of data exploration.

00:53:12.810 --> 00:53:15.570
You're kind of stuck where a
lot of data scientists and data

00:53:15.570 --> 00:53:17.070
professionals are
spending more time

00:53:17.070 --> 00:53:21.675
managing their cluster than
they are doing data science.

00:53:21.675 --> 00:53:24.940
BigQuery is like renting
thousands of cores for seconds.

00:53:24.940 --> 00:53:27.630
You submit a job, it distributes
that job to thousands of cores,

00:53:27.630 --> 00:53:29.660
and you get answers back,
usually in seconds to minutes.

00:53:29.660 --> 00:53:32.490
And we've heard stories of folks
going from literally like, hey,

00:53:32.490 --> 00:53:34.650
we used to run
this 19 hour report

00:53:34.650 --> 00:53:36.870
and now we're down
to like 20 minutes.

00:53:36.870 --> 00:53:40.230
And so, if you're going to
take anything else away,

00:53:40.230 --> 00:53:41.939
figure out how you
can leverage BigQuery.

00:53:41.939 --> 00:53:43.563
And when you're in
BigQuery, everything

00:53:43.563 --> 00:53:45.510
looks like a BigQuery
problem, including ETL

00:53:45.510 --> 00:53:48.150
and including other things.

00:53:48.150 --> 00:53:51.720
For those processes that
don't lend themselves

00:53:51.720 --> 00:53:54.750
to go into BigQuery yet,
we have other things

00:53:54.750 --> 00:53:57.402
like a published subsystem
for events and whatnot.

00:53:57.402 --> 00:53:59.610
But if you're really stuck
and it's like no, no, I've

00:53:59.610 --> 00:54:01.525
got processes that are
built around Hadoop,

00:54:01.525 --> 00:54:04.230
then go back to the model
I talked about earlier.

00:54:04.230 --> 00:54:05.360
We have cloud data proc.

00:54:05.360 --> 00:54:08.220
So you can get a full Hadoop
cluster in 90 seconds.

00:54:08.220 --> 00:54:12.000
And so, you can schedule
Hadoop jobs, MapReduce jobs

00:54:12.000 --> 00:54:15.312
against your data as
individual pieces of work,

00:54:15.312 --> 00:54:17.520
where you could have that
data sitting inside of GCS.

00:54:17.520 --> 00:54:18.730
And then when it's
time to process,

00:54:18.730 --> 00:54:20.997
you kick off a cluster
[? via an API call, ?] process

00:54:20.997 --> 00:54:22.080
it, and then pull it back.

00:54:26.190 --> 00:54:28.100
The way that I hear
the BigQuery story told

00:54:28.100 --> 00:54:31.412
is when customers describe it
as democratizing data access.

00:54:31.412 --> 00:54:33.620
Because all of a sudden now
all his data scientists--

00:54:33.620 --> 00:54:35.965
or all their data scientists
were able to get at data

00:54:35.965 --> 00:54:37.340
and start running
ad hoc queries.

00:54:37.340 --> 00:54:40.059
And he's like, that unlocked
so much more value for us.

00:54:40.059 --> 00:54:41.600
And they
[? are an adtech ?] company.

00:54:41.600 --> 00:54:43.610
And so, being able
to start figuring out

00:54:43.610 --> 00:54:45.915
better segmentation and
running queries that just never

00:54:45.915 --> 00:54:48.290
were possible before made a
big change to their business.

00:54:53.530 --> 00:54:58.390
So I'll finish with this is that
you've heard a lot about how

00:54:58.390 --> 00:54:59.780
Google treats data.

00:54:59.780 --> 00:55:04.960
And that's because data is this
constant, virtuous cycle, where

00:55:04.960 --> 00:55:08.285
we see as customers can get
more and more in their data--

00:55:08.285 --> 00:55:09.910
and we've seen this
time and time again

00:55:09.910 --> 00:55:12.270
with folks using BigQuery
and our ML products,

00:55:12.270 --> 00:55:15.472
is that they spend a lot of
time taking care of their data.

00:55:15.472 --> 00:55:16.930
Because now they
can put it in GCS,

00:55:16.930 --> 00:55:19.040
and they can store forever, and
they can put it in BigQuery,

00:55:19.040 --> 00:55:21.280
and they can start running
queries and they never did.

00:55:21.280 --> 00:55:22.360
All of a sudden,
they're able to start

00:55:22.360 --> 00:55:23.915
deriving new business patterns.

00:55:23.915 --> 00:55:26.040
And that gives them insights,
and that turns around

00:55:26.040 --> 00:55:28.180
and plugs them right
back into innovation.

00:55:28.180 --> 00:55:30.070
It's thinking of new
products they can do,

00:55:30.070 --> 00:55:32.860
because they've got access and
insights from data they never

00:55:32.860 --> 00:55:34.060
had before.

00:55:34.060 --> 00:55:36.490
And once they've done
that, all of a sudden

00:55:36.490 --> 00:55:38.200
they're able to
craft new software.

00:55:38.200 --> 00:55:40.530
New software leads to more data.

00:55:40.530 --> 00:55:43.164
And so, it's this constant cycle
of a faster iteration cycle,

00:55:43.164 --> 00:55:44.455
and richer and better software.

00:55:48.312 --> 00:55:49.770
And there's a bunch
of talks on ML.

00:55:49.770 --> 00:55:51.770
So I would suggest
attending one of those

00:55:51.770 --> 00:55:54.010
to figure out what
the tweak is that ML

00:55:54.010 --> 00:55:56.654
is putting on the data spin.

00:55:56.654 --> 00:56:00.470
And with that, thank you.

